WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.402
This example creates a small contact table with several database constraints.

00:00:05.402 --> 00:00:08.852
The contact identifier is the primary key.

00:00:08.852 --> 00:00:13.287
Email is required, so it uses a not-null rule.

00:00:13.287 --> 00:00:18.757
A check constraint tests whether the email follows the selected structural pattern.

00:00:18.757 --> 00:00:25.067
Another check requires the phone, when present, to contain exactly ten digits.

00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:28.015
The email also has a unique constraint.

00:00:28.641 --> 00:00:31.718
The example then inserts one valid record.

00:00:31.718 --> 00:00:36.568
After you run the valid insert, try changing one thing at a time.

00:00:36.568 --> 00:00:44.198
For example, use an email that does not follow the expected pattern, or use a phone with too few digits.

00:00:44.198 --> 00:00:48.268
Postgres should reject values that violate the constraints.

00:00:48.268 --> 00:00:54.764
This is how a database can prevent some known quality problems before they become part of the stored data.
